@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# reaper v3.0.5 - Internet speed test launcher (the 10 Gbit/s field reports).
+#
+# (a) KEEP THE ENGINE'S STDERR. httpd starts the Ookla engine through _eval(),
+#     which redirects stdout only; every complaint the engine wrote to stderr
+#     was discarded, so a failing multi-gigabit run left nothing behind to
+#     read. $1 is the log path (one file per run, truncated each time).
+# (b) PRIORITY. During a multi-gigabit run Broadcom's real-time packet threads
+#     own the cores and the kernel logs "RT throttling activated"; the engine's
+#     own threads are SCHED_OTHER and were competing at nice 0 with httpd, the
+#     collectors and everything else in userspace. nice -10 keeps them ahead of
+#     that crowd without touching the RT budget (Tier 3 of the triage: never
+#     raise sched_rt_runtime_us).
+#
+# The engine's argv is passed through untouched - never re-parsed by a shell -
+# so a server id or interface name from the page cannot become a command.
+# `exec` keeps the pid, so httpd's pidof("ookla") / killall still see it.
+LOG="$1"; shift
+[ -n "$LOG" ] || LOG=/dev/null
+exec nice -n -10 ookla "$@" 2>"$LOG"
